```java
class Invoice {
    List<LineItem> items;
    double taxRate;

    double total() {
        double subtotal = items.stream().mapToDouble(LineItem::price).sum();
        return subtotal * (1 + taxRate);
    }
}
```

`total()` reads only `Invoice`'s own fields (`items`, `taxRate`) and needs nothing from outside. It belongs on `Invoice` — moving it anywhere else would mean either duplicating those fields elsewhere or reaching back into `Invoice` for them, both worse than leaving it where its data already lives.

```java
// Reaches into Invoice, Customer, and DiscountPolicy — belongs on none of them
double finalPrice(Invoice invoice, Customer customer, DiscountPolicy policy) {
    double base = invoice.total();
    double loyaltyBonus = customer.yearsActive() > 5 ? 0.05 : 0;
    return base * (1 - policy.rate() - loyaltyBonus);
}
```

This method doesn't primarily own any of the three classes' data — it's a coordination between them. The fix is extracting a new class, `PricingCalculator`, whose entire reason to exist is this calculation, rather than bolting it onto whichever of the three happened to be open first.
